WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.774
VRAM is normally local to each GPU.

00:00:02.774 --> 00:00:08.506
Installing two cards with 12 gigabytes each does not automatically create one transparent 24-gigabyte device.

00:00:08.506 --> 00:00:13.472
The runtime must support splitting or parallelism for a model to use both.

00:00:13.472 --> 00:00:16.911
Layer splitting places different model layers on different GPUs.

00:00:16.911 --> 00:00:19.203
A request moves through those layers,

00:00:19.203 --> 00:00:22.642
so the boundary between devices can add communication overhead.

00:00:22.642 --> 00:00:26.081
Uneven splits may be useful when memory sizes differ.

00:00:26.081 --> 00:00:30.283
Tensor parallelism divides mathematical operations across GPUs at a high level.

00:00:30.283 --> 00:00:34.868
It can use several devices simultaneously but requires synchronization and fast communication.

00:00:34.868 --> 00:00:38.307
Benefits depend on model, runtime, topology, and batch behavior.

00:00:38.307 --> 00:00:42.510
Model sharding is the broader idea of dividing weights or components.

00:00:42.510 --> 00:00:48.623
CPU offload can place some layers or cache in system memory when GPU capacity is insufficient,

00:00:48.623 --> 00:00:50.151
trading speed for fit.

00:00:50.151 --> 00:00:52.061
These methods are not equivalent.

00:00:52.061 --> 00:00:56.646
Context memory and key-value cache can be large, especially with long prompts,

00:00:56.646 --> 00:00:58.939
multiple sessions, or certain model architectures.

00:00:58.939 --> 00:01:05.052
A weight split that barely fits may leave no room for the intended context or concurrency.

00:01:05.052 --> 00:01:08.872
The smallest, slowest, or least-connected GPU can become a bottleneck.

00:01:08.872 --> 00:01:12.311
Balance should consider usable memory, compute, bandwidth, thermal behavior,

00:01:12.311 --> 00:01:13.457
and software capability.

00:01:13.457 --> 00:01:17.278
Benchmark the exact split rather than adding VRAM labels together.

00:01:17.278 --> 00:01:20.717
Cache placement can be as important as weight placement.

00:01:20.717 --> 00:01:25.683
Long context and simultaneous sessions may load one device unevenly or require transfers,

00:01:25.683 --> 00:01:28.740
so test the intended cache strategy and concurrency.
